# Keep the desktop on VMLord's output, and only on it.
#
# A Hyper-V guest has a synthetic display of its own -- simpledrm at first,
# hyperv_drm once that is unbound -- and a compositor that finds two cards
# lights both. The second monitor is one nobody can see: it is drawn on the
# Hyper-V console, not in the viewer's window. Worse than useless, because an
# absolute pointer is mapped across the whole desktop, so with a 1024x768
# monitor beside a 1920x1080 one the guest's cursor lands about a third to the
# right of where the user pointed and clicks miss whatever they aimed at.
# Task #121 measured exactly that on a live guest.
#
# `mutter-device-ignore` is the tag mutter already reads for a card that is not
# a display -- 61-mutter.rules uses it for vkms -- and this file sorts after
# that one so the tag is added rather than overwritten.
#
# Which makes this one of two ways rather than the way. The tag is a word only
# mutter reads, so the agent installs this file where the desktop it found is
# GNOME and installs `vmlord-display-unbind-hyperv.service` instead where it is
# not -- unbinding the card being what hides it from a compositor that reads no
# such tag. Exactly one of the two is ever on a guest.
#
# The TEST is what keeps this from taking the only display a guest has: with no
# vmlord_drm loaded -- a payload that failed to build, a kernel it will not
# compile against -- the Hyper-V card stays exactly what it was, and the guest
# has a desktop on the console instead of none at all.
SUBSYSTEM=="drm", KERNEL=="card[0-9]*", DRIVERS=="hyperv_drm", \
    TEST=="/sys/devices/platform/vmlord_drm.0/drm", TAG+="mutter-device-ignore"
